{
  "decision": "patch",
  "limitations": [
    "Only the three listed grounding entries are repaired using supplied source. Authored content remains unchanged; these proposed patches establish no semantic equivalence, completeness, acceptance, authority, qualification or execution."
  ],
  "patches": [
    {
      "family": "concepts",
      "grounding_index": 0,
      "reason": "Restores exact source whitespace and anchors F11's scope, instance premises and commitment standing.",
      "record_key": "change_management_foundation_family",
      "replacement": {
        "end_line": 788,
        "quote": "This family (F11 in the index of\n[FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundation-to-law))\ndecides how a change enters the chain, how its classification is computed\nand reused, how ambiguity is governed and how a shared interface is\nmigrated. Its instance premises, which the commitments do not supply, are\nnamed in the rows as follows: changing specification, the specification\nchanges while work proceeds; one front door, reports of every kind arrive\nat one product; shared interfaces, a shared interface has several\nproducers and consumers; reserved decisions, a decision may be taken\nunder declared policy or be reserved to a human authority. Standing of\nthe commitments cited (FOUNDATIONS §3): P0 recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and\nP6 independent; P4b derived from P0 and P2, with P0's premise that\nstep-produced facts are not re-acquirable; P5 derived from P0 and P6,\nwith P0's open-world premise.\n",
        "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20change%20management%20and%20ambiguity",
        "start_line": 775
      }
    },
    {
      "family": "concepts",
      "grounding_index": 0,
      "reason": "Corrects the section-ID transcription error and cites the passage supporting comment purpose, location and adoption-dependent authority.",
      "record_key": "ticketing_comment",
      "replacement": {
        "end_line": 78,
        "quote": "The product definition's `ticketing.comments.root` binding is the message\nboard; `.ai-workspace/comments/` is the default. Comments are for publishing\nideas, strategy notes, reviews, handoffs, closure summaries, and decisions\nwith their reasoning. Comments are not task status authority. A comment\nis a post of [POSTING_GUIDE](POSTING_GUIDE.md), which states what it\ncarries, its structure and its discussion state. A decision recorded in a\ncomment takes its authority from the surface that owns the decision, once\nit is adopted there ([POSTING_GUIDE §9](POSTING_GUIDE.md#9-ratification)),\nand has none of its own (P6, P3; rc.2 #comments).\n",
        "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F1.%20Work%20carriers%2FComments",
        "start_line": 70
      }
    },
    {
      "family": "residuals",
      "grounding_index": 0,
      "reason": "Restores exact whitespace and includes both the non-human definition and the person-under-policy clause underlying the retained uncertainty.",
      "record_key": "probabilistic_processing_actor_wording",
      "replacement": {
        "end_line": 1228,
        "quote": "Lawful probabilistic processing\n(`urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:lawful-probabilistic-processing`)\nis bounded non-human processing permitted by declared policy to carry or\nresolve declared ambiguity. Human adjudication\n(`urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication`) is explicit\nhuman judgment admitted by authority to resolve declared ambiguity where\npolicy or risk appetite requires it. That admission makes an operation\nhuman adjudication. A person who relays, records or carries out a step\ndoes not adjudicate by doing so, and a person who carries or decides a\ndeclared ambiguity where policy permits it, without that admission, acts\nunder the policy and recording that govern lawful probabilistic\nprocessing; who performs an operation does not decide its kind (§11, rule\n2). The default governance model: ambiguity\n",
        "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
        "start_line": 1216
      }
    }
  ],
  "refusal_reason": ""
}
